## Test Infrastructure

- **Elevated build-start hang (fixed 2026-08-22).** The elevated `native-coverage.ps1`
  wrapper appeared to hang at build start: a bare `MSBuild.exe` invocation failed to
  resolve on PATH in the fresh elevated shell (machine/user PATH lacks MSBuild), the
  resulting CommandNotFound exception aborted the child script before the `.done`
  marker was created, and the parent shell polled for that marker forever. Fixed by
  resolving MSBuild via `vswhere` in `scripts/native-coverage.ps1` plus wrapping the
  child script invocation in try/catch/finally in
  `.claude/scripts/native-coverage-elevated.ps1`, so a terminating error still creates
  the marker. A full elevated run after the fix (679 tests, 13 minutes) completed
  cleanly with no post-test hang.

- **Admin test runs hang on exit (historic; not reproduced in the last two attended
  runs).** `run-coverage.ps1` (full mode) or `native-coverage.ps1` launched elevated
  via UAC has intermittently failed to exit cleanly after `dotnet test` reports all
  tests passed, with the parent shell sitting indefinitely on `Start-Process -Wait`.
  This is a different failure mode than the build-start hang above (it happens after
  tests pass, not at build start). Not reproduced in the 2026-07-13 or 2026-08-22
  attended runs - two clean runs, not proof the underlying issue is gone. If it
  recurs, prior investigation narrowed the suspects to:
  - `WatchUsnJournal_*` admin tests use `CancelIoEx` to unblock a kernel wait -
    possible the cancel races with process teardown and leaves a worker thread in a
    non-signaled wait, blocking the runtime's exit.
  - The `dotnet test` host process (`testhost.dll`) running under instrumentation may
    not flush the coverage file before the parent reaps it.
  - Long-term remedy if it recurs: split admin-only tests into a separate test
    project so the non-admin run stays the default and elevated runs can be opted
    into with a known-stable test set.

